 Full-bleed immersive chapters may intentionally occupy the full viewport with open edges. A major change from a framed chapter to full-bleed media should have a deliberate spatial handoff rather than an abrupt scale jump. Any entry motion should be brief, preserve the established story motion, avoid per-frame layout changes, and collapse to complete static content for reduced-motion and no-JS.
 
+Repeated chapters may share an entry-frame language when one intentionally transforms out of it. Use one width and corner authority for the shared entry; spatial continuity does not require identical final containers. Validate matching visible edges and radius at entry, and the intended open edges after expansion.
+
 Use the space to compose media, headings and narrative content, rather than adding empty padding. Tablet major chapters should retain comparable visual authority. Mobile may use an optically smaller sticky stage so surrounding content and browser chrome remain usable; ordinary content chapters use a minimum height and must grow with their content. Viewport-sized storytelling never authorizes clipped content or trapped scrolling.
 
 Chapter-to-chapter whitespace is narrative rhythm, not leftover space. Give a major chapter a perceptible ending and the next a deliberate entry through section spacing, alignment or background transitions. Choose the actual spacing through full-page and transition viewport QA, not a universal fixed gap or blank spacer elements. Reduced-motion and no-JS modes must keep complete content without retaining a long motion-only rail.
 
